WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.930
You're choosing where to run an inference
workload.

2
00:00:03.597 --> 00:00:06.937
An hourly GPU price tells you what
capacity costs.

3
00:00:07.557 --> 00:00:11.231
The useful question is how much accepted
work that capacity delivers.

4
00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:15.677
Here are two retained runs of the same
scheduled coding workload:

5
00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:18.247
8,622 requests in each.

6
00:00:18.277 --> 00:00:20.117
Open the acceptance contract.

7
00:00:20.737 --> 00:00:23.617
An answer counts when it passes the
recorded correctness checks,

8
00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:26.847
its first token arrives within one second
of scheduled arrival,

9
00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:28.477
and it finishes within sixty.

10
00:00:29.097 --> 00:00:31.464
Every failed or late request stays in the
record.

11
00:00:32.577 --> 00:00:37.882
Under the original grading, the MI300X
accepted 4,336 requests.

12
00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:41.381
The H100 accepted 4,280.

13
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.270
Using this scenario's list-price
assumptions,

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.358
that's about $0.74 and $1.20 per thousand
accepted requests.

15
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.920
Now change the H100's hourly rate to
$2.49.

16
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.520
This is an explicit price scenario.

17
00:00:56.744 --> 00:00:59.134
Save the change. The earlier decision
becomes stale.

18
00:00:59.164 --> 00:01:02.967
Its inputs and conclusion remain visible
until we explicitly recompute.

19
00:01:04.084 --> 00:01:05.574
Run the comparison again.

20
00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:10.404
The H100 now costs about $0.68 per
thousand accepted requests.

21
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:13.631
It becomes the preferred option under
these requirements.

22
00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.700
The captured responses and their grades
stay the same.

23
00:01:22.304 --> 00:01:25.094
Retain this edition. Both decisions stay
in the history,

24
00:01:25.124 --> 00:01:27.974
with the assumption that changed and the
evidence each one used.

25
00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:29.034
Export the workspace.

26
00:01:29.064 --> 00:01:31.394
Open the same packet in a fresh offline
client.

27
00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.734
It verifies the packet and recalculates

28
00:01:33.764 --> 00:01:35.724
the decisions from their retained inputs.

29
00:01:36.824 --> 00:01:39.104
A new price can reuse existing
measurements.

30
00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:41.734
A new model, runtime, hardware
configuration

31
00:01:41.764 --> 00:01:44.144
or workload needs new observations.

32
00:01:44.776 --> 00:01:46.854
Provider reliability and complete
operating costs

33
00:01:46.884 --> 00:01:48.544
still require their own evidence.

34
00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.406
Inspect this example in your browser,

35
00:01:51.436 --> 00:01:54.336
then run the kit against your own
endpoints and acceptance criteria.
